<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The TSX closed June 8 at 34,479, up 0.19% on a day when Israel and Iran exchanged missiles for the first time since the April ceasefire. Energy names led gains. The market discounted both the attack and the partial de-escalation announced by midday; the shaded band marks the two-session period when the exchange occurred.</p>

<h2>The Advisor''s Structural Advantage in This Moment</h2>

<p>The availability heuristic creates a specific and recurring advisory opportunity. Clients whose judgment is distorted by vivid recent imagery need exactly what a skilled advisor provides: a calibrated reassessment of actual probability, not emotional reassurance. The difference matters because reassurance without calibration does not change the cognitive distortion. It simply suppresses it temporarily, leaving the underlying bias intact for the next triggering event.</p>

<p>Calibration means offering the client the data that competes with the imagery for cognitive space. Monday''s close. The behaviour of professional markets. The historical pattern of geopolitical shocks and market recoveries. The specific, concrete question: what did markets price on the day this happened, and what does that tell you about the actual risk assessment of the most informed participants in the global economy?</p>

<p>This is not cheerleading. It is cognitive rebalancing. The client who receives this framing does not simply feel better. They have a new, accurate data point competing with the missile footage for availability. The next time a similar event occurs, the competing data point is also available. Over time, the pattern of "vivid event, markets process and recover" becomes part of the client''s mental model, reducing the advisor''s intervention burden with each iteration.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients are processing a specific cocktail this morning: residual fear from vivid weekend imagery, confusion about why markets did not collapse the way they expected, and a low-level anxiety about what comes next. The confusion is worth noting, because clients who expected a market crash and did not get one do not necessarily feel relieved. They often feel unsettled, as though they missed something. That cognitive dissonance is the primary emotional state to address. Secondary: clients with energy exposure may feel briefly validated by Monday''s gains but uncertain whether to act on it. Clients with heavier equity allocations may be quietly wondering whether to reduce exposure before what they expect will be the "real" market reaction.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I want to talk to you about what happened over the weekend, because I think the way most people processed it was different from what the data actually showed.

Israel and Iran exchanged strikes Sunday night. It was serious, and the footage was alarming. But here is what markets did: the TSX closed Monday up 0.19%. Energy names led gains. Canadian Natural, Suncor, Cenovus all finished higher. By mid-Monday, Iran had suspended operations and both sides were signalling a pause.

The gap between what that looked and felt like and what markets actually did is not unusual. It is a documented pattern in behavioral finance called the availability heuristic. When something is vivid and recent and emotionally intense, we naturally judge it as more probable and more dangerous than the evidence warrants. The missile footage was about as vivid and emotionally charged as news gets. Markets, which are priced by professional participants with access to real intelligence and diplomatic channels, made a different assessment.

The more structurally important event this week is the Bank of Canada decision tomorrow morning. That one does not come with dramatic footage, but it will affect your mortgage rate, your bond holdings, and your planning horizon in ways that Sunday night''s exchange probably will not. That is what I want to make sure we are positioned for.

Can we spend ten minutes going through your rate-sensitive holdings and what tomorrow''s decision might mean for your situation specifically?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients who are news consumers and check their portfolios frequently. Clients approaching retirement with concentrated equity exposure. Clients who have raised the subject of reducing equity in prior recent conversations and now feel their concern is validated.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with energy holdings who are watching the TSX energy sector gains with satisfaction but remain uncertain about duration. Clients in balanced portfolios who are absorbing mixed signals from equity performance and bond yield moves ahead of the BoC decision.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients who hold overweight TSX energy positions established during the Hormuz-driven run. Clients who receive this reframe and begin to internalize the availability heuristic pattern as a permanent feature of their investment framework, reducing future intervention needs.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify the two to three clients most likely to call today based on prior anxiety signals. Reach out proactively before they call you.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a one-paragraph written summary of Monday''s TSX close and energy sector performance to share with clients who ask about the weekend. Specific numbers reduce availability distortion faster than narrative reassurance.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag clients with upcoming portfolio reviews and note the BoC decision context for those conversations. The rate-sensitive discussion is the substantive one this week.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document any client who expresses intent to reduce equity exposure. Confirm their stated risk tolerance matches their current allocation. If it does not, this is the conversation to have before a market event forces it.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review client exposure to TSX energy sector names. Clients who are meaningfully overweight may need a rebalancing conversation framed around discipline rather than the weekend''s events.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> What the weekend events actually meant for your portfolio<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
I wanted to follow up on our conversation about the weekend''s news. The Israel-Iran exchange was serious and the coverage was alarming, but markets provided an important calibration: the TSX closed Monday up 0.19%, with energy names posting gains of 1.4% to 1.7% as the situation de-escalated through the day.<br><br>
This pattern, vivid geopolitical event followed by a composed market response, is well-documented. Professional markets priced both the attack and the subsequent de-escalation quickly. The more consequential event for your portfolio this week is likely the Bank of Canada''s rate decision Wednesday morning, which will provide clearer guidance on the interest rate environment for the remainder of 2026.<br><br>
I will be in touch after the BoC announcement with a brief note on what it means for your situation specifically. In the meantime, please do not hesitate to reach out if you have questions.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors with heavy equity exposure:</strong> The weekend events exposed a specific vulnerability in self-directed portfolios: no one to call, no framework for separating the emotional signal from the portfolio signal. These investors are exactly the people who are most likely to make a poorly timed decision today, and most likely to be receptive to a conversation that offers them a better framework.</p>
<p><strong>Recently retired or near-retirement professionals:</strong> The sequence shock risk is most acute for this group. A client who is one or two years into drawdown is particularly susceptible to the availability heuristic driving a decision to reduce equity that they will regret when markets recover.</p>
<p><strong>Energy sector employees or former employees:</strong> This group has direct, personal exposure to the geopolitical story. Their understanding of the sector is real but their emotional engagement with it is higher than average, making them more susceptible to availability distortion in both directions, excessive optimism on the way up, excessive fear on the way down.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am reaching out because I wanted to offer some context on what happened over the weekend before markets open this morning. The news was alarming, but the market response told a different story, and I think it is worth two minutes of your time before you make any decisions about your portfolio today.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The moment after a vivid geopolitical event is precisely when the gap between managing your own portfolio and having professional guidance is largest. A DIY investor this morning is navigating missile footage, social media commentary, cable news analysis, and a portfolio dashboard, all simultaneously, with no framework for prioritizing which information actually matters for their financial plan.</p>
<p>An advisor who reaches out this morning with specific data, Monday''s TSX close, the energy sector performance, the BoC decision on the horizon, is offering something no algorithm and no news outlet is delivering: calibrated, individualized context for a single client''s specific situation. That is the value that cannot be replicated by a robo-advisor or a self-directed account.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How are you feeling about your portfolio after the weekend''s news?</p>
<p>Have you made any changes to your investments in the last few days?</p>
<p>When you look at your current allocation, does the equity exposure feel right given what you''ve been seeing in the news?</p>
<p>What would need to be true about your portfolio for you to feel confident enough to stop monitoring it daily?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> What markets actually told us about the weekend<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
I wanted to share a quick data point in case it is useful as you process the weekend''s news. Despite Israel and Iran exchanging strikes for the first time since the April ceasefire, the TSX closed Monday up 0.19%. Energy names led gains. Markets priced both the attack and the partial de-escalation quickly.<br><br>
This gap between how alarming an event feels and how markets actually respond is a documented pattern in behavioral finance. Managing it well is one of the most concrete things a financial advisor does for clients, particularly during periods when the news is moving faster than most people can rationally process.<br><br>
If you are managing your own portfolio and would find it useful to talk through how to think about geopolitical risk in the context of your specific situation, I would be glad to have that conversation. No obligation.<br><br>
[Your Name] | [Firm] | [Contact]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

