<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The CRA prescribed rate for income-splitting loans peaked at 6% in Q3 2023 through Q2 2024 before falling back to 3% in Q3 2025, where it has held for five consecutive quarters. The green band marks the current 3% window; Q4 2026 is not yet set and will be determined by July T-bill yields, which are sensitive to tomorrow''s Bank of Canada statement tone.</p>

<h2>The TFSA and RRSP Angle That Is Often Missed</h2>

<p>Clients who are focused on the prescribed rate loan conversation sometimes miss a related planning question: asset location inside the income-splitting structure. The invested funds advanced through a prescribed rate loan should be held in non-registered accounts on the borrower''s side, because the income from those investments is what gets taxed at the lower rate. If the same funds were inside a TFSA or RRSP, the income-splitting benefit still exists but the additional registered account tax shelter means the advisor may be double-stacking tax efficiency in one account while leaving taxable room elsewhere underutilized.</p>

<p>The sequencing question for high-net-worth clients this summer is: TFSA and RRSP room first, prescribed rate loan for the excess, and non-registered equity held on the lower-income spouse''s side in the loan structure. That ordering maximizes after-tax returns across the family unit and creates the most flexibility at the eventual drawdown stage.</p>

<p>For CCPC clients, the calculus has an additional layer. Investment income inside a corporation is subject to the refundable dividend tax on hand (RDTOH) mechanism, and the prescribed rate loan can move passive investment income outside the corporation and into the hands of a lower-taxed family member. With the BoC signalling that a rate hike is more likely than a cut over the next two decisions, the window to establish these structures at 3% may be shorter than clients realize.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Most clients are not tracking prescribed rate announcements. The ones who have existing structures are in one of two states: confident (they set it up properly and have been paying the annual interest on time) or anxious (they vaguely remember setting something up but are not sure whether the January 30 payment was made). The anxious group is the higher-priority conversation. Clients who have not yet set up a prescribed rate loan are typically unaware that a time-sensitive window exists. Bringing this to their attention is a value-delivery moment that most will not have received from any other source.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I want to make sure you are aware of something that is time-sensitive before tomorrow''s Bank of Canada decision.

The CRA just confirmed the prescribed rate for income-splitting loans holds at 3% for the third quarter, July through September. That rate has been at 3% for five straight quarters, which is the most favourable it has been since late 2022. Here is the important part: Q4 is not yet set. It gets calculated from July T-bill yields, and those yields are sensitive to what Governor Macklem says tomorrow morning.

If the Bank signals that a rate hike is more likely than not in the second half of the year, which the April statement already started to suggest, the Q4 prescribed rate almost certainly goes to 4%.

What that means for you: any prescribed rate loan you establish before Q3 ends locks in at 3% for the life of the loan, even if the CRA rate goes to 4%, 5%, or higher in future quarters. A loan established after Q3, under a 4% regime, would require paying 4% annually to maintain the attribution exemption. On a $500,000 loan, that is $5,000 annually versus $15,000 under the peak rate we saw in 2024.

I want to make sure we have reviewed whether this is the right year to put a structure in place, or whether your existing structure is in good standing. Can we schedule 30 minutes this week?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> High-income professionals with a lower-income spouse who do not yet have a prescribed rate loan in place. CCPC owners with passive investment income inside the corporation and family members in lower brackets. Clients with family trusts where the loan component has not been reviewed recently.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with existing prescribed rate loans who are current on all interest payments. They are locked at their original rate and the Q4 announcement does not change their situation. However, they may benefit from a review of the asset location within the structure.</p>
<p><strong>Action required:</strong> Any client with a prescribed rate loan who did not clearly pay the January 30, 2026 interest deadline. This is the advisory emergency in the portfolio. Attribution failures compound; catching them early limits the damage.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull the list of clients with existing prescribed rate loan structures. Confirm in writing with each that the January 30, 2026 interest payment on the 2025 loan balance was made before the deadline.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify high-income clients with a lower-income spouse who do not yet have a prescribed rate loan. Flag these for a mid-June conversation before Q3 ends.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For CCPC clients with passive investment income inside the corporation, review whether a prescribed rate loan structure would move investment income outside the corp into lower-taxed family hands more efficiently.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">After tomorrow''s BoC statement, assess the tone for hike signals. If language has shifted hawkish, advance the prescribed rate loan conversations from mid-June to immediately.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Confirm that clients with existing structures have the loan documented in a formal written agreement. Verbal structures do not satisfy the CRA requirement regardless of how scrupulous the interest payments have been.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Prescribed rate loan update: window and next steps<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
I wanted to flag a time-sensitive planning item following the CRA''s Q3 2026 prescribed rate announcement. The rate for income-splitting loans has been confirmed at 3% through September 30. The Q4 rate is not yet set and will be determined by July T-bill yields.<br><br>
The Bank of Canada decides tomorrow. If the statement signals a hike is more likely than a cut for the second half of 2026, the Q4 prescribed rate will likely rise to 4%. Any prescribed rate loan established during Q3 locks in at 3% permanently, regardless of future CRA rate changes. A loan established after Q3 at a higher rate increases the annual interest cost required to maintain the attribution exemption.<br><br>
For your existing structure, I want to confirm the January 30, 2026 interest payment was made on time. Please let me know if you have any uncertainty about this. For clients who do not yet have a prescribed rate loan in place, I would like to review whether this summer is the right window to establish one.<br><br>
I will be in touch after tomorrow''s BoC announcement with an update on what the statement means for the Q4 rate outlook.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice. Tax planning strategies should be reviewed with a qualified tax advisor.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>High-income professionals with non-working or lower-income spouses:</strong> The prescribed rate loan is one of the most powerful income-splitting tools available and it is chronically underutilized outside of HNW advisory practices. A physician, engineer, or senior executive whose spouse earns significantly less is the canonical candidate. Many have never been told this structure exists.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners approaching partial exit or significant liquidity event:</strong> These clients will have a compressed window post-transaction to place capital efficiently. Establishing a prescribed rate loan structure now, while the rate is 3%, means that structure is already in place when the liquidity arrives.</p>
<p><strong>DIY investors managing family wealth across multiple accounts:</strong> The complexity of asset location, prescribed rate loans, and attribution rules is exactly the kind of problem that is nearly impossible to optimize without professional guidance. This is a concrete demonstration of advisor value on a dollar-quantifiable basis.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am reaching out because there is a time-sensitive planning window that closes before the end of September, and I want to make sure you have the full picture before the Bank of Canada''s rate decision tomorrow changes the outlook for what comes next.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The prescribed rate loan structure is one of the few legal income-splitting mechanisms that remains fully available to Canadian investors after the capital gains inclusion rate debate and the ongoing policy environment. A $500,000 loan established at 3% and invested in a diversified equity portfolio returning 7% annually shifts approximately $35,000 per year in investment income from a top-bracket earner to a lower-bracket family member. At a 20-percentage-point marginal rate difference, that is $7,000 per year in annual tax savings, compounding for the life of the loan.</p>
<p>The advisor who brings this conversation to a client who has not had it before is delivering a quantifiable annual value that exceeds a typical advisory fee on its own. That is the prospecting proposition: not general wealth management, but a specific, named, time-sensitive opportunity with a concrete dollar figure attached.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Is there a significant income difference between you and your spouse or partner?</p>
<p>Do you have investment assets outside of registered accounts that are generating taxable income?</p>
<p>Has anyone reviewed your family''s overall tax exposure across all accounts and household members recently?</p>
<p>Do you have a formal income-splitting arrangement in place, and if so, when was it last reviewed?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> A planning window that closes before October<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
I wanted to reach out about a time-sensitive planning opportunity. The CRA''s prescribed rate for income-splitting loans has been confirmed at 3% through September 30. The Q4 rate is not yet set, and with the Bank of Canada''s rate decision tomorrow, it may rise to 4%.<br><br>
If there is a significant income difference between you and your spouse, a prescribed rate loan established before September 30 locks in the 3% rate permanently, regardless of future CRA increases. The structure can shift investment income from a high-bracket earner to a lower-bracket family member, generating ongoing annual tax savings that compound for as long as the loan remains in place.<br><br>
This is a straightforward calculation. If you would find it useful to see whether the structure makes sense for your situation, I would be glad to walk through the numbers with you at no obligation.<br><br>
[Your Name] | [Firm] | [Contact]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice. Tax planning strategies should be reviewed with a qualified tax advisor.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

