<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">WTI rose from $64 per barrel before the February 28 conflict to above $103 in late April before retreating to near $89 as ceasefire talks periodically improved sentiment. The April 8 ceasefire produced a sharp pullback; the June 7-8 exchange between Israel and Iran briefly pushed the price back above $94. The $90 support level has held as a floor through the ceasefire period.</p>

<h2>The Trade the TSX Has Priced, and What Unwinds It</h2>

<p>The IEEFA published analysis this week quantifying the Canadian energy windfall from the Hormuz closure. WCS rose from approximately $50 to above $90 per barrel, nearly 80%. WTI rose from $64 to a peak above $103 before settling near current levels. The TSX energy sub-index has tracked those price moves closely, and the broader TSX has outperformed most developed market peers over the same period precisely because of its energy and materials composition.</p>

<p>The asymmetric risk now runs in the direction that clients are least likely to be worried about. A further escalation from current levels, say another exchange of strikes or a US strike on Iranian energy infrastructure, would likely add $5 to $10 to WTI on a temporary basis. Barclays research cited in the IEEFA analysis estimates that a full Hormuz reopening would return roughly 14 million barrels per day of supply. Applied to current demand, that implies a supply-demand realignment that would push WTI materially lower, potentially toward the $70 to $75 range that J.P. Morgan cited in its longer-term forecast.</p>

<p>Bob Parker of the International Capital Markets Association, writing for CNBC last week, estimated that even a partial Hormuz reopening would push oil toward $90 per barrel by year end because of the infrastructure damage, inventory depletion, and security risk premium that would persist even after a deal. That is the base case for a peace scenario: a significant but not catastrophic decline from current levels. The tail risk for Canadian energy portfolios is a comprehensive deal with rapid reopening and swift inventory rebuild, a scenario that markets would price in well before tankers actually start moving.</p>

<p>The helicopter crash this morning and the fragility of the ceasefire framework suggest the deal risk is not imminent. But it is closer than it was in April, and the negotiating dynamic has shifted. Trump has said he may have an idea for a deal within days. Iran''s foreign affairs apparatus has said it has no problem with negotiations provided the US demonstrates good faith. The crash complicates that signalling on both sides.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with TSX energy exposure are in an unusual emotional state: they have been rewarded by the very news events that create anxiety. Energy stocks are up sharply, but the reason they are up is a conflict with ongoing escalation risk. Clients who benefit financially from elevated oil prices and are simultaneously disturbed by the conflict itself may feel a kind of cognitive dissonance that a good advisor can name and address. A different group of clients, those without meaningful energy exposure who have underperformed the TSX, may be experiencing regret and considering a reallocation at precisely the wrong moment in the trade cycle.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I want to make sure you are thinking about the right risk in your energy exposure, because it is the opposite of what most people are focused on.

Most of the attention right now is on escalation: what happens if the ceasefire breaks down entirely, if the US strikes Iranian infrastructure again, if oil spikes further. That is not a low-probability outcome, but for Canadian energy names, another escalation from here would likely add something in the range of $5 to $10 to WTI for a few weeks and then fade. Your portfolio has already absorbed the bulk of the price move that accompanied the shock. The upside from further escalation is marginal compared to what has already been realized.

The trade your portfolio is currently running is a bet that the Hormuz closure continues. That is a plausible base case. But the scenario that would materially change your energy position is a durable peace deal, which would return approximately 14 million barrels per day of Persian Gulf supply to the market and move WTI lower. It would not necessarily crash oil prices because of infrastructure damage, inventory depletion, and a lingering security premium. But it would reduce them meaningfully.

I am not suggesting we reduce energy exposure today. I am suggesting we agree in advance on what the signal is that would prompt a rebalancing conversation, so we are not making that decision reactively when it happens.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with overweight TSX energy allocations established in 2025 or early 2026. These clients have substantial unrealized gains that a peace deal would erode. The conversation is about managing a winning position, not a troubled one.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients in balanced Canadian equity funds with natural TSX sector weights. The TSX Composite energy sub-index weighting means any Canadian equity holder has energy exposure. A peace deal affects balanced portfolios through the sector weight, but the offset from financials and other sectors recovering makes the net impact more muted.</p>
<p><strong>Potential concern:</strong> Clients who have been underweight energy and are now considering adding exposure at current levels. This conversation requires careful framing: adding TSX energy exposure today means buying into a trade that has already run 66%, with the primary risk being the resolution of the underlying supply disruption.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review energy sector weighting across client portfolios. Flag any client whose energy allocation has drifted materially above their target weight due to price appreciation. These are rebalancing candidates, not necessarily sell signals.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Define, in writing for each relevant client, the oil price level or news event that would trigger a rebalancing conversation. Doing this now, when the trade is profitable and emotions are manageable, produces better outcomes than doing it reactively.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients who are considering adding energy exposure now. Prepare a framing note that contextualizes the 66% twelve-month gain and the asymmetric risk profile of the current moment before that conversation.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Monitor Trump''s statements on Iran negotiations over the next 48 hours. He has signalled a possible deal idea could emerge within days. A credible peace announcement would move oil faster than any other current catalyst.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Your energy positions and the risk that actually matters<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
I wanted to share a brief note on how I am thinking about the geopolitical situation and your portfolio. The weekend''s Israel-Iran exchange and this morning''s US helicopter crash near the Strait have kept oil near $89 after a brief spike above $94 Monday. The ceasefire is holding, barely.<br><br>
Your energy positions have performed well through this period because the Hormuz closure removed Persian Gulf supply from global markets and Canadian producers have captured the resulting price premium. The primary risk going forward is not another escalation, which would likely add modest and temporary upside. The primary risk is a durable peace deal, which would return significant supply and move oil prices lower, though probably not below $80 near-term given infrastructure damage and depleted inventories.<br><br>
I want to make sure we have agreed on what the signal is that would prompt us to review your energy allocation, so we can make that decision deliberately rather than reactively. Can we schedule a brief call this week?<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Alberta-based clients and former energy sector employees:</strong> These clients have concentrated personal and professional exposure to oil prices. They tend to have strong views on the conflict and its duration. A conversation that offers a calibrated framework for the peace scenario, rather than confirming their energy-sector bias, is both valuable and differentiated.</p>
<p><strong>DIY investors who added energy exposure in early 2026:</strong> These clients captured significant gains and may have no framework for managing a winning position. The question of when to reduce, how much to reduce, and what to do with the proceeds is precisely the kind of complex, multi-variable problem where advisor value is highest.</p>
<p><strong>Clients nearing retirement with concentrated Canadian equity exposure:</strong> For a client with a $1.5M portfolio that is 35% Canadian equity, the TSX energy sector''s 66% gain has meaningfully distorted their intended allocation. The rebalancing conversation is urgent but has to be handled carefully given the psychological difficulty of selling a winner.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I wanted to reach out because most of the conversation about the Middle East is focused on escalation risk, and I think the more important portfolio question right now is actually the opposite: what happens to your energy holdings if the deal comes together.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A self-directed investor who has benefited from energy sector gains over the past year faces a specific problem that emotional decision-making handles poorly: managing a profitable position in an environment where the positive catalyst, the Hormuz closure, could reverse at any time without warning. The instinct is to hold because the position has been right. The discipline is to identify in advance what the exit signal is and to have the rebalancing mechanism ready before the news arrives.</p>
<p>An advisor who has this conversation proactively, before the deal news arrives, is delivering a service that cannot be replicated by a robo-platform or a brokerage app. The value is not the decision itself. It is the framework that allows the decision to be made calmly and deliberately rather than reactively and emotionally.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How much of your portfolio is currently in Canadian energy names, either directly or through Canadian equity funds?</p>
<p>Have you thought about what you would do with your energy holdings if a peace deal was announced tomorrow?</p>
<p>When your energy positions have appreciated significantly, what is your usual approach to rebalancing?</p>
<p>Is there a price level for WTI or a news event that would change how you are thinking about your Canadian equity allocation?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The risk in the energy trade that nobody is talking about<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
With oil near $89 and the TSX energy sector up 66% over twelve months, most of the conversation is about what happens if the Middle East escalates further. I think the more important question for Canadian portfolios is the opposite: what happens if a deal is reached?<br><br>
A durable US-Iran peace agreement with Hormuz reopening would return approximately 14 million barrels per day of supply. Oil prices would decline meaningfully from current levels, though probably not collapse given infrastructure damage and depleted inventories. The TSX energy sector, which has been the primary driver of Canadian equity outperformance, would reprice.<br><br>
Managing a winning energy position in advance of a potential catalyst is exactly the kind of portfolio problem where a second opinion is most valuable. If you would like to talk through how to think about your Canadian equity exposure in that scenario, I am glad to connect.<br><br>
[Your Name] | [Firm] | [Contact]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
