<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The TSX (solid, left axis) and GoC 5-year yield (dashed, right axis) tracked in loose inverse relationship through the period until Friday June 5, when strong Canadian and US jobs data drove a simultaneous yield spike and equity selloff. The 0.19% Monday recovery restored the geopolitical fear premium but not the Friday decline. The yield at 3.16% remains the active variable entering tomorrow''s BoC decision.</p>

<h2>Tuesday''s Positioning Before the BoC</h2>

<p>Markets open Tuesday with three simultaneous inputs: the US Army helicopter incident near the Strait of Hormuz this morning (cause unknown, crew safe), WTI at $89 after Monday''s spike-and-retreat, and a BoC decision at 9:45 AM tomorrow that will be read primarily through the Friday jobs data lens rather than the geopolitical lens.</p>

<p>The jobs beat changed the Bank''s calculus in a specific way. The April MPR had projected the Canadian economy growing 1.2% in 2026, with the unemployment rate remaining in the 6.5% to 7% range. An 88,000-job May print, if not revised significantly lower, sits in significant tension with that unemployment forecast. Either the labour market is stronger than the Bank modelled or the job gain is concentrated in sectors that will not persist, which requires a different analytical answer.</p>

<p>The TSX energy sub-index remains up 66% over twelve months. WTI near $90 continues to provide the fundamental support for that sector performance. The financials recovery Monday, with RBC, TD, and BMO each up roughly 1%, reflected the stabilisation of bond yields after Friday''s spike, not a new catalyst. The index as a whole enters Tuesday positioned primarily to receive tomorrow''s BoC statement, which is the dominant event of the week for Canadian equity.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who looked at their portfolios Monday and saw a partial recovery will likely not understand the distinction between what recovered and what did not. The geopolitical fear from the weekend exchange is gone. The rate anxiety from Friday''s jobs print is not, and it is more consequential for most Canadian portfolios. Clients with fixed-rate mortgage renewals in the next twelve months, or with bond-heavy allocations, are sitting in a yield environment that moved materially against them on Friday and has not fully reversed. That is the conversation that requires proactive outreach, not Monday''s headline recovery number.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I want to make sure you understand what actually happened in markets last week, because the Monday recovery number can be misleading.

Friday''s 2.3% TSX decline, the biggest single-day drop since February, was not driven by the Middle East. It was driven by Canada adding 88,000 jobs in May, which is a strong number that pushed bond yields higher and repriced rate expectations. The GoC 5-year yield moved to 3.16%, which matters directly for fixed mortgage rates.

Monday recovered the fear from the weekend strikes, not Friday''s yield-driven decline. The TSX is still about 800 points below where it was the Thursday before last.

Tomorrow''s Bank of Canada decision is the key event. The rate holds at 2.25%, that is certain. What matters is the language in the statement. If Macklem acknowledges that the May jobs data changes the near-term outlook and keeps the door open for a hike, yields will likely move higher again. If the statement emphasizes Q1 GDP weakness and signals ongoing caution, yields may ease. Either way, your mortgage renewal situation and your bond allocation need to be understood in the context of where the 5-year yield settles after tomorrow.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with fixed-rate mortgage renewals in the next twelve months. The GoC 5-year yield at 3.16%, and the nesto forecast suggesting it could reach 3.70% by year end, implies renewal rates meaningfully above current outstanding mortgages. The window for rate holds and advance renewals is active now.</p>
<p><strong>Significant impact:</strong> Clients with materials sector exposure concentrated in gold miners. Agnico Eagle, Barrick, and Wheaton each fell more than 7% Friday and did not fully recover Monday. The gold price decline from the Fed hike expectations repricing is a distinct mechanism from the energy sector''s Hormuz-driven support.</p>
<p><strong>Monitoring required:</strong> Clients with balanced Canadian equity exposure who have benefited from the TSX energy run but whose materials allocation has been dragging. The sector divergence within the TSX is widening, and rebalancing conversations may be warranted if energy weight has drifted significantly above target.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Read the BoC statement at 9:45 AM tomorrow. The primary market signal: whether the language acknowledges the May jobs beat and revises the unemployment outlook, and whether the two-way rate risk formulation from April is maintained or strengthened.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients with mortgage renewals in the next twelve months. The 5-year yield at 3.16% and the upside risk to 3.70% by year end makes a rate-hold conversation urgent for this group.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review gold miner exposure in client materials allocations. The Friday selloff in Agnico, Barrick, and Wheaton was mechanical and acute. Confirm whether the weight now warrants rebalancing or whether the thesis for gold at $4,300 remains intact after the Fed repricing.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Do not conflate Monday''s 0.19% recovery with a resolution of Friday''s underlying driver. Document for client files that the rate anxiety from the jobs print remains live entering the BoC decision.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> What Monday''s TSX recovery actually means<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
The TSX closed Monday up 0.19%, which likely reads as a recovery from last week. It is worth being precise about what recovered. Monday''s gain reversed the geopolitical fear premium from the weekend Israel-Iran exchange, which was the right market response as both sides stepped back from further escalation.<br><br>
Friday''s 2.3% decline, however, was driven by something different: Canada added 88,000 jobs in May and US payrolls also beat expectations. The resulting bond yield spike, with the Government of Canada 5-year yield moving to 3.16%, repriced rate expectations and hit rate-sensitive sectors hard. That decline has not been recovered. The GoC 5-year yield remains at 3.16%, up from near 3.00% in mid-May.<br><br>
Tomorrow''s Bank of Canada decision at 9:45 AM is the key event for the week. I will be in touch after the statement with a read on what it means for your situation.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
