  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Mortgage renewal clients at other institutions:</strong> A blowout jobs print, a rising 5-year yield, and a BoC decision tomorrow is precisely the environment in which self-directed mortgage decisions are most likely to be suboptimal. A client comparing renewal rates without a framework for interpreting the yield curve direction is flying blind in a market that moved materially last week.</p>
<p><strong>Retired investors with gold miner exposure:</strong> The Friday selloff in Agnico, Barrick, and Wheaton affected a specific type of investor disproportionately: retirees with Canadian materials allocation who held those names for yield or as inflation protection. The mechanism that drove the decline, rising US rate expectations, is unlikely to reverse quickly. The conversation is about whether the original thesis remains intact or whether the position should be reviewed.</p>
<p><strong>Busy professionals who check their portfolios weekly:</strong> This cohort will look at Friday''s decline and Monday''s partial recovery and draw incomplete conclusions. A proactive call with a precise explanation of what drove each move, and what tomorrow''s BoC means for their specific holdings, is the highest-value advisory action available this week.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I wanted to call before tomorrow''s Bank of Canada decision because there is something important about last week''s market moves that the Monday recovery number obscures, and it is directly relevant to your portfolio.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The sequence of events last week, a jobs beat, a yield spike, an equity selloff, a geopolitical exchange, a partial recovery, and a BoC decision pending, is exactly the kind of multi-factor environment in which a self-directed investor is most likely to draw the wrong conclusion and act on it. The 0.19% Monday recovery looks like "it all worked out." The 2.3% Friday decline and the GoC 5-year yield at 3.16% say something different about the rate environment going into the rest of 2026.</p>
<p>An advisor who calls this week with a precise account of what happened and what it means for a specific client''s mortgage, bond, and equity exposure is providing something no brokerage app or news feed delivers. The value is not the market call. It is the translation from market event to financial plan implication.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>When you looked at your portfolio Monday, what did you conclude about what happened last week?</p>
<p>Do you have a fixed-rate mortgage coming up for renewal in the next year, and have you been following what the 5-year bond yield has been doing?</p>
<p>How much of your Canadian equity exposure is in materials names, and do you understand what drove that sector''s underperformance Friday?</p>
<p>Are you watching tomorrow''s Bank of Canada decision, and if so, what outcome are you expecting?</p>
